for the first time in decades hira lala  is feeling optimistic  the kashmiri hindu fled with his family  from indian administered kashmir in 1990  because of religious violence he's  pleased with the indian government's  decision to revoke the region's autonomy  under article 370 but has his concerns  if we are we are supposed to go back  to there where shall we leave where is  our home  what is our livelihood what shall we  earn  and shall we be saved there  who will look after us and who is  who will be a governor for our safety  there  india's home minister led tuesday's  charge for change in the lower  house of parliament where the bill  passed easily thanks to the government's  majority  and support from the opposition members  but many members of parliament say  support for india in the kashmir valley  has dried up  following the government's revoking of  the region's autonomy and splitting it  into two federal territories  even those who politically supported the  indian government in the region are now  lashing out  this is not india that i believe in  my india is india  or india for all whatever your religion  whatever your region these students  studying near new delhi are worried  they've been scrambling to find a way to  contact their families and friends in  indian administered kashmir  without much success my main concern  that are my parents even okay  like are they even i don't know are they  even alive  being away from home and not knowing  what they are going through  and when you have been a part of it  already in the past so i know what are  the problems  but this is the like highest this is the  worst kind of situation that has  happened in kashmir till now  as many worry about their families and  kashmir being cut off  others are dreaming of a new beginning  there  is jameel al-jazeera new delhi
 

